## Chromecast with Google TV vs. Original Chromecast: An Important Distinction

Before anything else, let's clear up a common point of confusion. There are two very different kinds of Chromecast devices, and understanding which one you have determines whether this guide applies to you.

| Device | Year Introduced | Has Google TV UI | Can Be Controlled as a Remote |
|---|---|---|---|
| Chromecast (1st–3rd gen) | 2013–2018 | No | ❌ No |
| Chromecast Ultra | 2016 | No | ❌ No |
| Chromecast with Google TV (HD/4K) | 2020–2023 | Yes | ✅ Yes |

The original Chromecast devices are **casting targets** — you push content to them from your phone using the Cast button in apps like YouTube or Netflix. They don't have a standalone operating system or home screen UI. You can't navigate them with a remote because there's nothing to navigate.

**Chromecast with Google TV** is a completely different product. It runs the full Google TV interface, has a home screen, an app store, and a physical remote in the box. Most importantly, it supports the Android TV Remote Protocol, which means phone apps can control it over Wi-Fi — just like any other Android TV device.

This guide is for **Chromecast with Google TV** owners. If you have an original Chromecast, this won't apply.

---

## What Is the Android TV Remote Protocol?

The Android TV Remote Protocol (sometimes called the ATVR protocol) is a network-based control standard built into Android TV and Google TV. It operates over your local Wi-Fi network using mDNS for device discovery and a TCP socket connection for sending commands.

Google uses this same protocol for the official Google TV app's remote feature. Because it's a documented protocol, third-party developers can also implement it — giving you access to cleaner, faster, and more customizable remote apps.

When you use a phone app to control your Chromecast with Google TV, here's what happens under the hood:
1. The app broadcasts a discovery packet on your local network
2. The Chromecast responds with its device name and IP address
3. Your phone initiates a secure connection; the TV shows a PIN
4. You enter the PIN; the connection is established and saved
5. Button presses from the app are sent as JSON commands over TCP

